      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in Portland Cement Concrete (PCC) pavements is often driven by thermal stresses that prevent the slab from expanding freely as temperatures rise. As the concrete heats up, thermal expansion combined with high bitumen viscosity in the joint sealant restricts deformation, leading to internal tensile stresses that exceed the material's tensile strength. This phenomenon is frequently exacerbated if the subgrade lacks sufficient stability to distribute these loads evenly or if joint construction does not allow for the necessary movement, resulting in stress ribbing along the expansion joints.",
